.TH LAC 1 "August 2026" "Linux Admin Center 1.3.0-alpha4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lac \- modular Linux desktop administration toolkit
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lac
.RI [ OPTION ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Linux Admin Center (LAC) provides an interactive terminal interface and
command-line options for common Linux desktop administration tasks.
.PP
Run
.B lac
without an option to open the interactive menu. Exactly one command-line option
may be supplied at a time.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h , " --help"
Show command-line help.
.TP
.BR \-v , " --version"
Show version information.
.TP
.BR \-i , " --system-info"
Show system information.
.TP
.BR \-s , " --storage-analysis"
Show read-only capacity and inode analysis for local persistent filesystems.
.TP
.BR \-n , " --network-info"
Show network information.
.TP
.BR \-r , " --network-diagnostics"
Show read-only network diagnostics.
.TP
.BR \-d , " --hardware-diagnostics"
Show read-only hardware diagnostics.
.TP
.BR \-g , " --gaming-readiness"
Show the gaming readiness overview.
.TP
.BR \-G , " --gaming-diagnostics"
Show detailed gaming diagnostics.
.TP
.BR \-e , " --service-health"
Show service and boot health information.
.TP
.BR \-S , " --self-check"
Check the LAC runtime, launchers, configuration access and dependencies.
.TP
.BR \-u , " --check-updates"
Check for available system package updates.
.TP
.BR \-c , " --cleanup-report"
Show a read-only system cleanup report.
.SH STORAGE ANALYSIS
The
.BR \-s , " --storage-analysis"
option reads mounted local filesystem statistics through
.BR df (1).
For every included filesystem it reports the source, type, mount point, used,
total and available capacity, capacity percentage, and inode usage when that
measurement is applicable.
.PP
Capacity and inode usage are assessed independently. Usage below 80 percent is
.BR healthy ,
usage from 80 percent is a
.BR warning ,
and usage from 90 percent is
.BR critical .
The most severe filesystem result determines the overall assessment. Missing
inode measurements are shown as not applicable. Missing or unreadable
filesystem data, or no analyzable persistent local filesystem, produces an
.B incomplete
assessment.
.PP
The report explains every assessment state and displays state-specific next
steps. Recommendations distinguish capacity pressure from inode pressure,
refer to the read-only
.B lac --cleanup-report
output where applicable, and advise creating a backup before cleanup, resizing
or storage expansion. Recommendations do not perform any modifying action.
.PP
An elevated
.I /recovery
filesystem retains its measured assessment, but the report explains that high
usage can be expected when recovery installation media is stored there. If
only this filesystem triggers a warning or critical result, generic cleanup
and archival guidance is replaced with advice to use the distribution's
supported recovery or update tools and never delete recovery files manually.
.PP
Pseudo-filesystems, remote mounts, known read-only image filesystems and
selected virtual FUSE filesystems are excluded. The implementation relies on
the GNU Coreutils output options of
.BR df (1)
available on the supported Debian, Fedora, Arch Linux and openSUSE targets.
.PP
Storage Analysis does not use
.BR sudo (8),
mount or unmount filesystems, run repair, trim or resize commands, calculate
directory hotspots, delete data or perform storage maintenance. Physical drive
health remains part of Hardware Diagnostics. Confirmed cleanup actions remain
part of System Cleanup.
.SH CONFIGURATION
LAC reads system configuration from
.I /etc/lac/lac.conf
and user configuration from an explicit
.B LAC_USER_CONFIG
path, the XDG configuration directory, or
.IR ~/.config/lac/lac.conf .
.PP
The packaged example configuration is installed under
.IR /usr/share/linux-admin-center/lac.conf.example .
.SH PACKAGE MANAGEMENT
Debian package installations are managed through
.BR apt (8)
and
.BR dpkg (1).
The package intentionally does not install a
.B lac-uninstall
command. Remove it with:
.PP
.nf
sudo apt remove linux-admin-center
.fi
.PP
Configuration under
.I /etc/lac
and user configuration are not removed by that operation.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/lac
Installed command.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/linux-admin-center/
LAC runtime files.
.TP
.I /usr/share/linux-admin-center/
Shared files and example configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/lac/lac.conf
Optional system-wide configuration.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR apt (8),
.BR df (1),
.BR dpkg (1)
